<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_document" style="font-size:173%;">Kalman Bayesian Transformer
</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Haoming Jing
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Oren Wright
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">José M. F. Moura
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yorie Nakahira
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>The authors are with the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, United States.
<span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">{haomingj, omw, moura, ynakahir}@andrew.cmu.edu</span></span></span></span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_funding ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>This work is sponsored in part by the PRESTO Grant Number JPMJPR2136 from the Japan Science and Technology Agency, in part by the National Science Foundation under Grants No. 2442948 and CCF-2327905, and in part by the Department of the Navy, Office of Naval Research, under award number N00014-23-1-2252. The views expressed are those of the authors and do not reflect the official policy or position of the US Navy, Department of Defense, or the US Government.</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Sequential fine-tuning of transformers is useful when new data arrive sequentially, especially with shifting distributions. Unlike batch learning, sequential learning demands that training be stabilized despite a small amount of data by balancing new information and previously learned knowledge in the pre-trained models. This challenge is further complicated when training is to be completed in latency-critical environments and learning must additionally quantify and be mediated by uncertainty.
Motivated by these challenges, we propose a novel method that frames sequential fine-tuning as a posterior inference problem within a Bayesian framework. Our approach integrates closed-form moment propagation of random variables, Kalman Bayesian Neural Networks, and Taylor approximations of the moments of softmax functions. By explicitly accounting for pre-trained models as priors and adaptively balancing them against new information based on quantified uncertainty, our method achieves robust and data-efficient sequential learning. The effectiveness of our method is demonstrated through numerical simulations involving sequential adaptation of a decision transformer to tasks characterized by distribution shifts and limited memory resources.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Efficient fine-tuning of transformer models has become increasingly important in machine learning applications. While pre-trained transformer models demonstrate remarkable performance across various tasks, they often experience degradation when deployed on data distributions that differ from their training sets. In these scenarios, full retraining can be prohibitively expensive, highlighting the need for lightweight, parameter-efficient fine-tuning methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. However, existing approaches typically require significant computational resources.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, data may arrive sequentially, and the onboard hardware may have limited memory to store all past data. In such scenarios, it is desirable to perform fine-tuning sequentially. Unlike batch learning, sequential learning requires the model to incorporate new information while preserving knowledge from past data. This balance is nontrivial to realize when only small amounts of data are available at each training step, amplifying the instability of training and the risk of catastrophic forgetting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These challenges are further compounded by the need for quantifying uncertainty. Overconfident predictions can lead to catastrophic outcomes, particularly when data is limited or the system is intrinsically noisy. Incorporating uncertainty estimation (e.g., taking a Bayesian approach) also adds to the computational burden, making these methods even less tractable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Bayesian approaches for transformer fine-tuning have emerged as promising alternatives that can explicitly incorporate prior knowledge and uncertainty <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. However, conventional techniques for Bayesian deep learning—such as variational inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>—often involve computationally intensive iterative optimization with extensive sampling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we formulate fine-tuning as a posterior inference problem within a Bayesian framework and introduce the <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Kalman Bayesian Transformer</span>. Central to our method is a novel integration of closed-form characterization of neural network moments, Kalman Bayesian Neural Networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, with a Taylor approximation of the distribution of a softmax function. Our method has the following advantages.</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sequential learning</span>: Our method explicitly incorporates previously trained parameters as a prior and balances the prior with new samples based on the size of uncertainty. This enables stable sequential training, which avoids the latency of waiting for future data and reduces onboard memory requirements (Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ V-B Results and Analyses ‣ V Numerical Simulation ‣ Kalman Bayesian Transformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computational efficiency</span>: Unlike traditional Bayesian methods that rely on expensive iterative sampling-based evaluations, our method evaluates closed-form formulas in a single pass, which significantly reduces the computational requirements (Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ V-B Results and Analyses ‣ V Numerical Simulation ‣ Kalman Bayesian Transformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Explicit uncertainty quantification</span>: Our method quantifies the uncertainties in the prediction, which informs prediction confidence and enhances robustness against noisy data (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Forward Pass ‣ IV Proposed Method ‣ Kalman Bayesian Transformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related work</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training transformers using Bayesian approaches.</span> Various Bayesian approaches have been developed for transformers. Some methods focus on learning all parameters with quantified uncertainties for training, while others explore parameter-efficient techniques for fine-tuning.
For example, Bayesian Transformer introduces a full Bayesian learning framework for transformers used in language modeling, which quantifies uncertainty and works with limited data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. BayesFormer uses a dropout-based method for transformers that approximates variational inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.
On the other hand, parameter-efficient techniques are studied, which aim to improve retraining efficiency by modifying only a subset of the transformer’s parameters. For instance, BayesTune <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> employs a Laplace prior on each parameter to identify which parameters to update based on posterior estimates, and uses MCMC methods to draw samples from the posterior.
BALM (Bayesian Active Learning with pre-trained language models) uses Monte Carlo dropout to approximate uncertainty, which is used in active learning to select data to annotate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
Bayesian low-rank adaptation uses Laplace approximation to estimate the posterior over adaptation parameters, demonstrating reduced overconfidence in models fine-tuned on small datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
These approaches span a range of Bayesian techniques, ranging from variational inference, to MCMC, Monte Carlo dropout, and Laplace approximation. However, the existing literature for transformer (re)training has not explored the techniques from Kalman estimators.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Moment propagation techniques.</span>
Various techniques have been proposed that propagate moments through nonlinear transformations in closed form <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. A deterministic version of stochastic variational inference, proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and expanded on by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, uses moment propagation to solve variational inference in closed form. These techniques can provide the theoretical foundation and computational tools for efficient (re)training and analysis techniques of neural networks. For example, they are used to train probabilistic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and fully Bayesian neural networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. In this paper, we use the moment propagation method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> to fine-tune transformers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bayesian neural networks.</span>
Bayesian deep learning, first proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, can represent model uncertainty and allow for models that are robust and data-efficient. In contrast to standard neural networks, which find a single configuration of parameters <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> based on training data <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, Bayesian neural networks model the full posterior probability distribution <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathcal{W}|\mathcal{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathcal{W}|\mathcal{D})</annotation></semantics></math>. This allows for the representation of predictive probability</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\mathcal{D})=\int_{\mathcal{W}}p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\mathcal{W})p(\mathcal{W}|\mathcal{D})d\mathcal{W}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\mathcal{D})=\int_{\mathcal{W}}p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\mathcal{W})p(\mathcal{W}|\mathcal{D})d\mathcal{W}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This predictive probability distribution, or Bayesian model average, accounts for epistemic uncertainty (analogous to measurement noise in control), i.e. the uncertainty over which parameter setting is correct given the observed data. This can be reduced with additional training data, in contrast to aleatoric uncertainty, which is inherent in the underlying data process (analogous to system noise in control) and is irreducible <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. However, exact Bayesian inference is too computationally expensive for modern neural networks, and extensive efforts have been made to develop approximate Bayesian techniques with improved computation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. Markov chain Monte Carlo sampling has been widely studied, and approximates the posterior by sampling from a Markov process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Variational inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and Laplace approximation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> use differential information to fit a Gaussian distribution to the posterior. Kalman smoothing is applied to train Bayesian neural networks using closed-form formulas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Techniques like dropout <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> and SWAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> can be used to cheaply approximate the variational distribution. We integrate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> with moment propagation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> and approximation techniques of distributions for a different problem, sequentially fine-tuning transformers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Problem Statement</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the problem of fine-tuning a transformer model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> in a supervised learning setting. Below, we introduce the notation, the transformer model, and the Bayesian fine-tuning problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation.</span>
We denote scalars, vectors, and matrices by <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Depending on the context, <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> may also denote a set of matrices and/or vectors. We use <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{0}_{d_{1}\times d_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{0}_{d_{1}\times d_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote a <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> matrix with all entries <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. We use <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{1}_{d_{1}\times d_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{1}_{d_{1}\times d_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote a <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> matrix with all entries <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. We use <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}_{d_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}_{d_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote a <math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> identity matrix. We may omit the subscript when the dimension is obvious. We use <math id="S3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>𝐯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the mean of a random vector <math id="S3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{v},\mathbf{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{v},\mathbf{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the covariance of a random vector <math id="S3.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{v}_{1},\mathbf{v}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{v}_{1},\mathbf{v}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the cross-covariance between two random vectors <math id="S3.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The variables <math id="S3.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> are exclusively used as indices, such as the lo op counter in the pseudocode. We use <math id="S3.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}[i,j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}[i,j]</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the real number at the <math id="S3.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th row and <math id="S3.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th column of a matrix <math id="S3.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, and use <math id="S3.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}[i]</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the real number at the <math id="S3.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th entry of a vector <math id="S3.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use <math id="S3.p2.m34" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{A}[i:j,k:l]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}[i:j,k:l]</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the submatrix that is formed by the rows <math id="S3.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> (inclusive) and columns <math id="S3.p2.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> (inclusive) of matrix <math id="S3.p2.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use <math id="S3.p2.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the ceiling and flooring functions on real numbers, <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</span> <math id="S3.p2.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil x\rceil=\min\{y\in\mathbb{Z}\mid y\geq x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil x\rceil=\min\{y\in\mathbb{Z}\mid y\geq x\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor x\rfloor=\max\{y\in\mathbb{Z}\mid y\leq x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor x\rfloor=\max\{y\in\mathbb{Z}\mid y\leq x\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10695v1/transformer_overview_v2.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/514;" width="476" height="514" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Proposed architecture. The original transformer architecture is shown on the left, and the Bayesian neural network that replaces the linear layer is shown on the right.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transformer.</span>
We consider a known and pre-trained transformer network which can be represented as a mapping <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:\mathbb{R}^{x\times d}\times\mathbb{R}^{y\times d}\rightarrow(0,1)^{y\times d_{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:\mathbb{R}^{x\times d}\times\mathbb{R}^{y\times d}\rightarrow(0,1)^{y\times d_{o}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is the length of the input sequence, <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the length of the output sequence, <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding dimension, and <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> is the output dimension. We separate the network into 2 parts, <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</span> <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=T_{2}\circ T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=T_{2}\circ T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}:\mathbb{R}^{x\times d}\times\mathbb{R}^{y\times d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{y\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}:\mathbb{R}^{x\times d}\times\mathbb{R}^{y\times d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{y\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the mapping from the input sequence and target sequence to the representation <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\text{dec}}=[\mathbf{h}^{i}_{1},\mathbf{h}^{i}_{2},\cdots,\mathbf{h}^{i}_{y}]^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mtext>dec</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\text{dec}}=[\mathbf{h}^{i}_{1},\mathbf{h}^{i}_{2},\cdots,\mathbf{h}^{i}_{y}]^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}:\mathbb{R}^{y\times d}\rightarrow(0,1)^{y\times d_{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}:\mathbb{R}^{y\times d}\rightarrow(0,1)^{y\times d_{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the mapping from the representation <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\text{dec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mtext>dec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\text{dec}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the output probability <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ III Problem Statement ‣ Kalman Bayesian Transformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Since the transformer outputs a probability distribution, we define a one-hot mapping <math id="S3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{one-hot}}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\{0,1\}^{d_{\text{world}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>one-hot</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>world</mtext></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{one-hot}}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\{0,1\}^{d_{\text{world}}}</annotation></semantics></math> that maps a vectorized token to a vector of <math id="S3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>s and a <math id="S3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, where the <math id="S3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> appears in the entry corresponding to the token in the dictionary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bayesian fine-tuning.</span>
The training data available for fine-tuning are <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{\{\mathbf{X}^{1},\mathbf{Y}^{1}\},\{\mathbf{X}^{2},\mathbf{Y}^{2}\},\cdots,\{\mathbf{X}^{N},\mathbf{Y}^{N}\}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{\{\mathbf{X}^{1},\mathbf{Y}^{1}\},\{\mathbf{X}^{2},\mathbf{Y}^{2}\},\cdots,\{\mathbf{X}^{N},\mathbf{Y}^{N}\}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of sequences available for fine-tuning. Regarding the <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th sequence, <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{x_{i}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{x_{i}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input sequence, and <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{y_{i}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{y_{i}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the output sequence. Here, <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the length of the input sequence, <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the length of the output sequence, and <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimension of the embedding. We assume that all sets of sequences in the training data are independent and identically distributed.
In the fine-tuning process, we freeze <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and replace the linear part in <math id="S3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with a Bayesian neural network with <math id="S3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers. A ReLU activation layer is placed in between 2 linear layers. Let <math id="S3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}^{i}=[\mathbf{u}^{i}_{1},\mathbf{u}^{i}_{2},\cdots,\mathbf{u}^{i}_{y}]^{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{y\times n_{i}},i\in\{1,2,\cdots,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}^{i}=[\mathbf{u}^{i}_{1},\mathbf{u}^{i}_{2},\cdots,\mathbf{u}^{i}_{y}]^{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{y\times n_{i}},i\in\{1,2,\cdots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the intermediate representation after the <math id="S3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th linear layer, and <math id="S3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}^{i}=[\mathbf{z}^{i}_{1},\mathbf{z}^{i}_{2},\cdots,\mathbf{z}^{i}_{y}]^{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{y\times n_{i}},i\in\{1,2,\cdots,L-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}^{i}=[\mathbf{z}^{i}_{1},\mathbf{z}^{i}_{2},\cdots,\mathbf{z}^{i}_{y}]^{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{y\times n_{i}},i\in\{1,2,\cdots,L-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the intermediate representation after the <math id="S3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th ReLU activation layer, where <math id="S3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of neurons in the <math id="S3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer. The inference process through the Bayesian neural network is given by</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{w}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{w}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=[(\mathbf{w}^{i}_{1})^{T},(\mathbf{w}^{i}_{2})^{T},\cdots,(\mathbf{w}^{i}_{n_{i}})^{T}]^{T}\sim p(\mathbf{w}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=[(\mathbf{w}^{i}_{1})^{T},(\mathbf{w}^{i}_{2})^{T},\cdots,(\mathbf{w}^{i}_{n_{i}})^{T}]^{T}\sim p(\mathbf{w}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{U}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{U}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=f_{1}(\mathbf{Z}^{i-1})[\mathbf{w}^{i}_{1},\mathbf{w}^{i}_{2},\cdots,\mathbf{w}^{i}_{n_{i}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=f_{1}(\mathbf{Z}^{i-1})[\mathbf{w}^{i}_{1},\mathbf{w}^{i}_{2},\cdots,\mathbf{w}^{i}_{n_{i}}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{Z}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{Z}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\max(\boldsymbol{0},\mathbf{U}^{i})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\max(\boldsymbol{0},\mathbf{U}^{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S3.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2,\cdots,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2,\cdots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the function <math id="S3.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> appends a vector of <math id="S3.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>s to account for the bias term. An illustration of this structure is given in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ III Problem Statement ‣ Kalman Bayesian Transformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The input <math id="S3.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐙</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to the network is <math id="S3.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\text{dec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mtext>dec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\text{dec}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the output is given by</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{P}=[\mathbf{p}_{1},\mathbf{p}_{2},\cdots,\mathbf{p}_{y}]^{T}=\text{softmax}(\mathbf{U}^{L})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{P}=[\mathbf{p}_{1},\mathbf{p}_{2},\cdots,\mathbf{p}_{y}]^{T}=\text{softmax}(\mathbf{U}^{L}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The objective is to computationally efficiently find the posterior distribution of the weights</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D})=\frac{p(\mathcal{D}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})}{p(\mathcal{D})}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D})=\frac{p(\mathcal{D}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})}{p(\mathcal{D})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}=\{\mathbf{w}^{1},\mathbf{w}^{2},\cdots,\mathbf{w}^{L}\}\in\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}=\{\mathbf{w}^{1},\mathbf{w}^{2},\cdots,\mathbf{w}^{L}\}\in\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of parameters in the Bayesian neural network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10695v1/layers_illustration.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/84;" width="476" height="84" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration for one layer of the Bayesian neural network ((<a href="#S3.E2" title="In III Problem Statement ‣ Kalman Bayesian Transformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III Problem Statement ‣ Kalman Bayesian Transformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Proposed Method</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, we use a Bayesian smoothing algorithm to approximate the neural network’s posterior distribution by propagating first- and second-order moments. Let <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{k}:=\{\{\mathbf{X}^{1},\mathbf{Y}^{1}\},\{\mathbf{X}^{2},\mathbf{Y}^{2}\},\cdots,\{\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}\}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{k}:=\{\{\mathbf{X}^{1},\mathbf{Y}^{1}\},\{\mathbf{X}^{2},\mathbf{Y}^{2}\},\cdots,\{\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}\}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have the following factorization of (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In III Problem Statement ‣ Kalman Bayesian Transformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k})=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k})=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{p(\mathcal{D}^{k}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})}{p(\mathcal{D}^{k})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{p(\mathcal{D}^{k}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})}{p(\mathcal{D}^{k})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\prod^{k}_{i=1}p(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{Y}^{i}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})}{\prod^{k}_{i=1}p(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{Y}^{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\prod^{k}_{i=1}p(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{Y}^{i}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})}{\prod^{k}_{i=1}p(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{Y}^{i})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\prod^{k-1}_{i=1}p(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{Y}^{i}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})p(\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})}{\prod^{k}_{i=1}p(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{Y}^{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\prod^{k-1}_{i=1}p(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{Y}^{i}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})p(\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})}{\prod^{k}_{i=1}p(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{Y}^{i})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{p(\mathcal{D}^{k-1}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})p(\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})}{\prod^{k}_{i=1}p(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{Y}^{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{p(\mathcal{D}^{k-1}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})p(\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})}{\prod^{k}_{i=1}p(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{Y}^{i})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\frac{p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k-1})p(\mathcal{D}^{k-1})}{p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})}p(\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})}{\prod^{k}_{i=1}p(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{Y}^{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\frac{p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k-1})p(\mathcal{D}^{k-1})}{p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})}p(\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})}{\prod^{k}_{i=1}p(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{Y}^{i})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k-1})p(\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})\prod^{k-1}_{i=1}p(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{Y}^{i})}{\prod^{k}_{i=1}p(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{Y}^{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k-1})p(\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})\prod^{k-1}_{i=1}p(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{Y}^{i})}{\prod^{k}_{i=1}p(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{Y}^{i})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k-1})p(\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})}{p(\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k})}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k-1})p(\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})}{p(\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In IV Proposed Method ‣ Kalman Bayesian Transformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) is due to the assumption that the training data are i.i.d. Therefore, from (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In IV Proposed Method ‣ Kalman Bayesian Transformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), we have</p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k})\propto p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k-1})p(\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k})\propto p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k-1})p(\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}|\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which indicates that, given the distribution <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, obtaining the distribution <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k})</annotation></semantics></math> only requires <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we can update the weight iteratively. Each update consists of 2 parts:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Forward Pass. The forward pass uses Bayesian filtering computes the distributions of the intermediate variables and output, <span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</span> <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{U}^{i}\}_{i\in\{1,2,\cdots,L\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{U}^{i}\}_{i\in\{1,2,\cdots,L\}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{Z}^{i}\}_{i\in\{1,2,\cdots,L\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{Z}^{i}\}_{i\in\{1,2,\cdots,L\}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, conditioned on <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}^{0}=\mathbf{H}_{\text{dec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐙</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mtext>dec</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}^{0}=\mathbf{H}_{\text{dec}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the weights <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Backward Pass. The backward pass uses Kalman smoothing to update the distributions of the intermediate variables and weights in a backward manner, <span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</span> from layer <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> to layer <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, given the target output as new measurement.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">For reasons of economy, we will omit bias terms in the remainder of this section without loss of generality, as a layer’s input and weight matrix can be straightforwardly augmented to encompass the bias.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Training Data Preprocessing</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the autoregressive nature of the transformer, each pair of training data <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}\},\mathbf{X}^{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{x_{k}\times d},\mathbf{Y}^{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{y_{k}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}\},\mathbf{X}^{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{x_{k}\times d},\mathbf{Y}^{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{y_{k}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> can be seen as generated by a sequential process of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}-1</annotation></semantics></math> steps, where the <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th step takes input <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}[1:i,1:d]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}[1:i,1:d]\}</annotation></semantics></math>, obtains output <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}</annotation></semantics></math> from the transformer, and uses <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{P}[i:i,1:d_{o}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}[i:i,1:d_{o}]</annotation></semantics></math> to determine the distribution of the <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-th element in the output<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
            When applicable, the first row in the output <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> always corresponds to the start-of-sequence (SOS). Therefore, in the first step (<math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math>), the second element in the sequence is generated.</span></span></span>. If each step is considered a training instance, then training instances are not i.i.d. The recursive Bayesian update (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In IV Proposed Method ‣ Kalman Bayesian Transformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) requires each update of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</span> each forward pass and backward pass, to take into account the complete pair of training data <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We therefore modify the data representation with Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Training Data Preprocessing ‣ IV Proposed Method ‣ Kalman Bayesian Transformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, which preprocesses the data such that the data within a training pair are converted to a single batched representation, independent from other batches, to be propagated in the forward pass and backward pass. Specifically, the input for each training instance is converted to the representation <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\text{dec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mtext>dec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\text{dec}}</annotation></semantics></math> (line 4), and the output is converted to the one-hot representation (line 6). The procedure returns the batched representations corresponding to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (line 9). Each sequence of augmented data can be treated as i.i.d. and (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In IV Proposed Method ‣ Kalman Bayesian Transformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) now holds.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Data Preprocessing</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
            trained transformer <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=T_{2}\circ T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=T_{2}\circ T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with weights

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">procedure</span> <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Preprocessing</span>(<math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              Initialize <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be empty <math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="alg1.l3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> matrices

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\cdots,y_{k}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\cdots,y_{k}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
               <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{H}}\leftarrow[\hat{\mathbf{H}}^{T},T_{1}(\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}[1:i,1:d])^{T}]^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{H}}\leftarrow[\hat{\mathbf{H}}^{T},T_{1}(\mathbf{X}^{k},\mathbf{Y}^{k}[1:i,1:d])^{T}]^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
               <span id="alg1.l6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{2,3,\cdots,i+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{2,3,\cdots,i+1\}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
                 <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{Y}}\leftarrow[\hat{\mathbf{Y}}^{T},f_{\text{one-hot}}(\mathbf{Y}^{k}[j:j,1:d]^{T})]^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>one-hot</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{Y}}\leftarrow[\hat{\mathbf{Y}}^{T},f_{\text{one-hot}}(\mathbf{Y}^{k}[j:j,1:d]^{T})]^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
               <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{H}},\hat{\mathbf{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{H}},\hat{\mathbf{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">procedure</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Forward Pass</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because the distribution of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in layer <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> only depends on the distribution of weights at layer <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and the output <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}^{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}^{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> from the previous layer, we can propagate the first and second moment (mean and variance) one layer at a time. We define the vectorization</p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{u}^{i}:=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{u}^{i}:=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[(\mathbf{u}^{i}_{1})^{T},(\mathbf{u}^{i}_{2})^{T},\cdots,(\mathbf{u}^{i}_{y})^{T}]^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[(\mathbf{u}^{i}_{1})^{T},(\mathbf{u}^{i}_{2})^{T},\cdots,(\mathbf{u}^{i}_{y})^{T}]^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{z}^{i}:=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{z}^{i}:=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[(\mathbf{z}^{i}_{1})^{T},(\mathbf{z}^{i}_{2})^{T},\cdots,(\mathbf{z}^{i}_{y})^{T}]^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[(\mathbf{z}^{i}_{1})^{T},(\mathbf{z}^{i}_{2})^{T},\cdots,(\mathbf{z}^{i}_{y})^{T}]^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{p}:=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{p}:=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[(\mathbf{p}_{1})^{T},(\mathbf{p}_{2})^{T},\cdots,(\mathbf{p}_{y})^{T}]^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[(\mathbf{p}_{1})^{T},(\mathbf{p}_{2})^{T},\cdots,(\mathbf{p}_{y})^{T}]^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for all layers <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Let</p>
<table id="S4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\mathbf{W}}^{i}=\begin{bmatrix}(\mathbf{w}^{i}_{1})^{T}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}&amp;\cdots&amp;\boldsymbol{0}\\
\boldsymbol{0}&amp;(\mathbf{w}^{i}_{2})^{T}&amp;\cdots&amp;\boldsymbol{0}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
\boldsymbol{0}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}&amp;\cdots&amp;(\mathbf{w}^{i}_{n_{i}})^{T}\end{bmatrix}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mtd><mtd><mn>𝟎</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>𝟎</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>𝟎</mn></mtd><mtd><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>𝟎</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>𝟎</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>𝟎</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\mathbf{W}}^{i}=\begin{bmatrix}(\mathbf{w}^{i}_{1})^{T}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}&amp;\cdots&amp;\boldsymbol{0}\\
\boldsymbol{0}&amp;(\mathbf{w}^{i}_{2})^{T}&amp;\cdots&amp;\boldsymbol{0}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
\boldsymbol{0}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}&amp;\cdots&amp;(\mathbf{w}^{i}_{n_{i}})^{T}\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The linear transformation can be written as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{u}^{i}=\text{diag}(\underbrace{\tilde{\mathbf{W}}^{i},\tilde{\mathbf{W}}^{i},\cdots,\tilde{\mathbf{W}}^{i}}_{y})\mathbf{z}^{i-1}:=\bar{\mathbf{W}}^{i}\mathbf{z}^{i-1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mi>y</mi></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{u}^{i}=\text{diag}(\underbrace{\tilde{\mathbf{W}}^{i},\tilde{\mathbf{W}}^{i},\cdots,\tilde{\mathbf{W}}^{i}}_{y})\mathbf{z}^{i-1}:=\bar{\mathbf{W}}^{i}\mathbf{z}^{i-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Assuming <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent, the forward pass through the linear layer <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{\mathbf{M}}^{i}\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{z}^{i-1}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{\mathbf{M}}^{i}\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{z}^{i-1}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{M}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{M}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the mean of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{W}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{W}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given</p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{u}^{i}[j]=(\mathbf{w}^{i}_{j-n_{i}\lfloor\frac{j}{n_{i}}\rfloor})^{T}\mathbf{z}^{i-1}_{\lceil\frac{j}{n_{i}}\rceil}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{u}^{i}[j]=(\mathbf{w}^{i}_{j-n_{i}\lfloor\frac{j}{n_{i}}\rfloor})^{T}\mathbf{z}^{i-1}_{\lceil\frac{j}{n_{i}}\rceil},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.6" class="ltx_p">the covariance <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be easily calculated with</p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,k]=\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{u}^{i}[j]\mathbf{u}^{i}[k]]-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j]\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,k]=\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{u}^{i}[j]\mathbf{u}^{i}[k]]-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j]\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[k]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.7" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j,k\in\{1,2,\cdots,n_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j,k\in\{1,2,\cdots,n_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{z}^{i-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{z}^{i-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{z}^{i-1},\mathbf{z}^{i-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{z}^{i-1},\mathbf{z}^{i-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{w}^{i},\mathbf{w}^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{w}^{i},\mathbf{w}^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. From <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, Theorem 1]</cite>, the forward pass through the ReLU layer <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S4.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{z}^{i}}[j]=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{z}^{i}}[j]=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j]\Phi\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j]}{\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,j]}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j]\Phi\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j]}{\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,j]}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,j]}\phi\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j]}{\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,j]}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,j]}\phi\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j]}{\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,j]}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{z}^{i},\mathbf{z}^{i}}[j,k]=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{z}^{i},\mathbf{z}^{i}}[j,k]=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Phi\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j]}{\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,j]}}\right)\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,k]\Phi\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[k]}{\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[k,k]}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Phi\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j]}{\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,j]}}\right)\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,k]\Phi\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[k]}{\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[k,k]}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\frac{1}{2\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,j]\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[k,k]}}\phi\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j]}{\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,j]}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\frac{1}{2\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,j]\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[k,k]}}\phi\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j]}{\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,j]}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,k]\phi\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[k]}{\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[k,k]}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[j,k]\phi\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}[k]}{\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{u}^{i}}[k,k]}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.8" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j,k\in\{1,2,\cdots,n_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j,k\in\{1,2,\cdots,n_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> are the Gaussian cumulative density function and Gaussian probability density function, respectively. For brevity (<a href="#S4.E23" title="In IV-B Forward Pass ‣ IV Proposed Method ‣ Kalman Bayesian Transformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) shows only a second-order expansion, but the covariance can be computed to arbitrary precision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. Because the softmax is a vector operation for which mean and covariance cannot be easily computed, we use a first-order Taylor approximation to compute the forward pass through the softmax layer. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> be the softmax function. When the variance is small, the first order Taylor expansion about <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{s}(\mathbf{u}^{L})\approx f_{s}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})+\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})(\mathbf{u}^{L}-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{s}(\mathbf{u}^{L})\approx f_{s}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})+\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})(\mathbf{u}^{L}-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.9" class="ltx_p">The approximated mean for <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}=f_{s}(\mathbf{u}^{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}=f_{s}(\mathbf{u}^{L})</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{p}}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>𝐩</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{p}}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[f_{s}(\mathbf{u}^{L})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[f_{s}(\mathbf{u}^{L})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[f_{s}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})+\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})(\mathbf{u}^{L}-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[f_{s}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})+\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})(\mathbf{u}^{L}-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{s}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{s}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.10" class="ltx_p">since <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{s}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{s}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> are not functions of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}(\mathbf{u}^{L})=\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}(\mathbf{u}^{L})=\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Jacobian of the softmax function<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
            With slight abuse of terms, we refer here the function that individually applies softmax to each sub-vector <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}^{L}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>L</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}^{L}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> before vectorization.</span></span></span>. Similarly, we have</p>
<table id="S4.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{p}}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{p}}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[(f_{s}(\mathbf{u}^{L})-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{p}})(f_{s}(\mathbf{u}^{L})-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{p}})^{T}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>𝐩</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>𝐩</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[(f_{s}(\mathbf{u}^{L})-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{p}})(f_{s}(\mathbf{u}^{L})-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{p}})^{T}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[(f_{s}(\mathbf{u}^{L})-f_{s}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}}))(f_{s}(\mathbf{u}^{L})-f_{s}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}}))^{T}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[(f_{s}(\mathbf{u}^{L})-f_{s}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}}))(f_{s}(\mathbf{u}^{L})-f_{s}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}}))^{T}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[(\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})(\mathbf{u}^{L}-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}}))(\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})(\mathbf{u}^{L}-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}}))^{T}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[(\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})(\mathbf{u}^{L}-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}}))(\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})(\mathbf{u}^{L}-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}}))^{T}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})\mathbb{E}[(\mathbf{u}^{L}-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})(\mathbf{u}^{L}-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})^{T}]\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})\mathbb{E}[(\mathbf{u}^{L}-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})(\mathbf{u}^{L}-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})^{T}]\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{L},\mathbf{u}^{L}}\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{L},\mathbf{u}^{L}}\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.11" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S4.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{L},\mathbf{p}}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{L},\mathbf{p}}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{L},\mathbf{u}^{L}}\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})^{T}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{L},\mathbf{u}^{L}}\mathbf{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{u}^{L}})^{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.12" class="ltx_p">We note that the accuracy of this approximation can be improved with the addition of higher order Taylor expansion terms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Backward Pass</span></h3>

<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.4" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span>1 </span>Weight Initialization</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The iterative update of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{k})</annotation></semantics></math> starts with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math>. We would like <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{0})=p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}|\mathcal{D}^{0})=p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> to replicate the behavior of the linear layer of the original transformer. To achieve that, we design <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\text{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> is almost deterministic (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{w}^{i},\mathbf{w}^{i}}=\epsilon\mathbf{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{w}^{i},\mathbf{w}^{i}}=\epsilon\mathbf{I}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2,\cdots,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2,\cdots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter that prevents singularity in matrix inversion), and the input <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to the network is preserved until the last layer, where the weight <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\text{O}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>O</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\text{O}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the linear layer in the transformer is applied. To preserve the input through the ReLU activation, we design the initial weight such that <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}^{i},\forall i\in\{1,2,\cdots,L-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}^{i},\forall i\in\{1,2,\cdots,L-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a positive copy and a negative copy of the input. In this way, both the positive and negative parts are preserved in <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{i},\forall i\in\{1,2,\cdots,L-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{i},\forall i\in\{1,2,\cdots,L-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> after passing through the ReLU layer. The two parts are then added together and split again in each layer until the weight <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\text{O}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>O</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\text{O}}</annotation></semantics></math> is applied in the final layer. Specifically, we set</p>
<table id="S4.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{1}_{1}},\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{1}_{2}},\cdots,\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{1}_{n_{1}}}]=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{I}_{d}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}_{d\times(n_{1}-2d)}&amp;-\mathbf{I}_{d}\\
\boldsymbol{0}_{1\times d}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}_{1\times(n_{1}-2d)}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}_{1\times d}\end{bmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{1}_{1}},\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{1}_{2}},\cdots,\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{1}_{n_{1}}}]=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{I}_{d}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}_{d\times(n_{1}-2d)}&amp;-\mathbf{I}_{d}\\
\boldsymbol{0}_{1\times d}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}_{1\times(n_{1}-2d)}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}_{1\times d}\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{i}_{1}},\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{i}_{2}},\cdots,\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{i}_{n_{1}}}]=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{i}_{1}},\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{i}_{2}},\cdots,\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{i}_{n_{1}}}]=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{I}_{d}\\
\boldsymbol{0}_{(n_{i-1}-2d)\times d}\\
-\mathbf{I}_{d}\\
\boldsymbol{0}_{1\times d}\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{I}_{d}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}_{d\times(n_{i}-2d)}&amp;-\mathbf{I}_{d}\end{bmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{I}_{d}\\
\boldsymbol{0}_{(n_{i-1}-2d)\times d}\\
-\mathbf{I}_{d}\\
\boldsymbol{0}_{1\times d}\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{I}_{d}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}_{d\times(n_{i}-2d)}&amp;-\mathbf{I}_{d}\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\forall i\in\{2,3,\cdots,L-1\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\forall i\in\{2,3,\cdots,L-1\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{L}_{1}},\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{L}_{2}},\cdots,\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{L}_{n_{1}}}]=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>L</mi></msubsup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{L}_{1}},\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{L}_{2}},\cdots,\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathbf{w}^{L}_{n_{1}}}]=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{I}_{d}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}_{d\times 1}\\
\boldsymbol{0}_{(n_{L-1}-2d)\times d}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}_{(n_{L-1}-2d)\times 1}\\
-\mathbf{I}_{d}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}_{d\times 1}\\
\boldsymbol{0}_{1\times d}&amp;1\end{bmatrix}\mathbf{W}_{\text{O}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>O</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{I}_{d}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}_{d\times 1}\\
\boldsymbol{0}_{(n_{L-1}-2d)\times d}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}_{(n_{L-1}-2d)\times 1}\\
-\mathbf{I}_{d}&amp;\boldsymbol{0}_{d\times 1}\\
\boldsymbol{0}_{1\times d}&amp;1\end{bmatrix}\mathbf{W}_{\text{O}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.4" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span>2 </span>Weight Update</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the Rauch-Tung-Striebel (RTS) smoother to iteratively update the estimated distribution for <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math> for each layer. During the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th update, the RTS equations at each layer <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{L,L-1,\cdots,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{L,L-1,\cdots,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> are given by</p>
<table id="S4.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{K}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{K}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{z}^{i}}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{z}^{i},\mathbf{z}^{i}}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{u}^{i},\mathbf{z}^{i}}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{z}^{i},\mathbf{z}^{i}}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mu}^{+}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mu}^{+}_{\mathbf{u}^{i}}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
